# Lesson 08 -- Groups, and the shape that gives them away.
#
# Meaning here: a GROUP is a monoid in which every element has an inverse.
# Closed, associative, an identity, inverses -- four laws, and the top of the
# ladder this repository climbs.
# Not to be confused with: "group" in the everyday sense of a collection. A
# group is a set AND an operation, and the operation is most of the content.
#
# The payoff of the whole sequence is one picture. A group's Cayley table is a
# LATIN SQUARE: every element appears exactly once in each row and once in each
# column. Nothing about the definition mentions rows or columns -- it falls out
# of having inverses.
#
# READ THAT DIRECTION CAREFULLY. Every group table is a Latin square; a Latin
# square need NOT be a group. Lesson 11 exhibits subtraction modulo 3, which is
# a Latin square with no identity and no associativity. The shape is NECESSARY,
# not sufficient -- which is exactly the kind of thing a pretty picture will
# talk you out of noticing if nobody says it.
#
# This lesson also shows that the shape does not determine the group. Z4 and
# the Klein four-group are both order 4, both abelian, both Latin squares, and
# they are not the same group.
#
# NOT a Web UI file. This is a CLI lesson: it uses include and write_text,
# neither of which the browser session has, and it ends in ok(...) so the
# suite can gate on it. See docs/viewing.md.
#
# visual: ascii, svg
# loops: 0
# complexity: n^2 for the Latin-square test, n^3 for the associativity beneath it

include "../../lib/algebra.mlpl"
include "../../lib/render.mlpl"

u:prolog({
    title: "LESSON 08 -- GROUPS, and the shape that gives them away",
    intro: "A group is a monoid where everything can be undone. The definition says nothing about rows or columns, yet every group's table turns out to be a Latin square -- each element exactly once per row and per column. This lesson derives that shape, checks it against a monoid that fails it, and then shows two different groups wearing it."
  })

# --- Two groups of order 4 --------------------------------------------------
def u:add_mod_4(x, y) {
  "Answer the sum modulo 4.";
  mod(x + y, 4)
}
def u:xor_of(x, y) {
  "Answer the bitwise exclusive-or, which realizes the Klein four-group on {0, 1, 2, 3}.";
  bxor(x, y)
}

z4 = {
  title: "Z4 under addition: a cyclic group",
  names: ["0", "1", "2", "3"],
  table: table(:u:add_mod_4, u:elements(4), u:elements(4))
}
kl = {
  title: "The Klein four-group: not cyclic",
  names: ["e", "a", "b", "c"],
  table: table(:u:xor_of, u:elements(4), u:elements(4))
}

u:show_table(z4)
print("")
u:show_laws(z4)
print("")
u:show_table(kl)
print("")
u:show_laws(kl)

# --- The Latin-square property ----------------------------------------------
# Row a contains element v exactly when a * b = v has a solution b. In a group
# it always does -- take b = a-inverse * v -- and the solution is unique, so
# every element appears exactly once. That is the whole derivation, and it is
# checkable as a count.
print("")
print("Z4    -- is a Latin square:", u:yes_or_no(u:is_latin(z4.table)))
print("Klein -- is a Latin square:", u:yes_or_no(u:is_latin(kl.table)))

# --- A monoid that fails it -------------------------------------------------
def u:larger_of(x, y) {
  "Answer whichever argument is larger. Associative and commutative, with the smallest element as its identity.";
  if lt(x, y) {
    y
  } else {
    x
  }
}
mx = {
  title: "max on {0, 1, 2, 3}: a monoid, not a Latin square",
  names: ["0", "1", "2", "3"],
  table: table(:u:larger_of, u:elements(4), u:elements(4))
}
cm = u:classify(mx.table)
print("")
print("max   -- monoid:", u:yes_or_no(cm.monoid), " group:", u:yes_or_no(cm.group),
  " Latin square:", u:yes_or_no(u:is_latin(mx.table)))
print("  Row 3 is 3, 3, 3, 3 -- one element four times and the others missing.")
print("  A missing element in a row is an equation a * b = v with no solution,")
print("  which is exactly what having no inverse means.")

# The property tracks group-ness across the structures BUILT SO FAR -- and that
# is a fact about these five tables, not a theorem. Lesson 11 breaks it with a
# sixth.
def u:winner_in_rps(x, y) {
  "Answer the winner of a Rock-Paper-Scissors matchup; a tie answers the shared choice.";
  if eq(mod(x - y + 3, 3), 1) {
    x
  } else {
    y
  }
}
def u:left_arg(x, y) {
  "Answer the first argument, ignoring the second. Left projection.";
  x
}
checked = [u:is_latin(z4.table), u:is_latin(kl.table), u:is_latin(mx.table),
  u:is_latin(table(:u:winner_in_rps, u:elements(3), u:elements(3))),
  u:is_latin(table(:u:left_arg, u:elements(3), u:elements(3)))]
grouped = [u:classify(z4.table).group, u:classify(kl.table).group,
  cm.group, 0, 0]
print("")
print("Latin square? Z4, Klein, max, rock-paper-scissors, left projection:")
print("  latin: ", checked)
print("  group: ", grouped)
print("  agree everywhere:", u:yes_or_no(equal(checked, grouped)))
print("  -- on THESE five. The agreement is not a theorem: a Latin square need")
print("     not be a group, and lesson 11 exhibits one that is not.")

# --- Same shape, different groups -------------------------------------------
# Both are Latin squares, both abelian, both order 4. They differ in how many
# elements undo themselves: Z4 has two, Klein has four. No relabelling can
# change that count, so no relabelling can turn one into the other.
z4_self = reduce_add(eq(u:diagonal(z4.table), u:identity_of(z4.table)))
kl_self = reduce_add(eq(u:diagonal(kl.table), u:identity_of(kl.table)))
print("")
print("self-inverse elements -- Z4:", z4_self, " Klein:", kl_self)
print("  A relabelling can rename elements but not create or destroy the")
print("  property a * a = e, so these two groups are genuinely different.")
print("  Lesson 11 turns that argument into a search over all permutations.")

# --- The diagrams -----------------------------------------------------------
a = u:write_cayley_svg("out/08-group-z4.svg", z4)
b = u:write_cayley_svg("out/08-group-klein.svg", kl)
c = u:write_cayley_svg("out/08-not-a-group.svg", mx)

u:epilog_open()

u:explain("out/08-group-z4.svg",
  "The 4x4 table for Z4. Rows and columns are 0, 1, 2, 3, cells coloured by result.")
u:note("THE LATIN SQUARE: look down any row, or across any column. Four cells, four")
u:note("different colours, every element present and none repeated.")
u:note("The white cross marks the identity's row and column, as in lesson 06.")
u:note("WHAT TO NOTICE: the colours march diagonally. Each row is the row above shifted")
u:note("one place, because adding 1 is what generates this group -- start at 0 and keep")
u:note("adding 1 and you visit all four elements before returning. That is what CYCLIC")
u:note("means, and the diagonal banding is what it looks like.")

u:explain("out/08-group-klein.svg",
  "The 4x4 table for the Klein four-group, same layout.")
u:note("Also a Latin square: four colours per row and per column, no repeats.")
u:note("WHAT TO NOTICE: no diagonal march. The colours are symmetric about BOTH")
u:note("diagonals, and the main diagonal is entirely the identity's colour -- every")
u:note("element undoes itself. There is no single element you can keep applying to")
u:note("reach the rest, so this group is not cyclic. Same shape, different group.")

u:explain("out/08-not-a-group.svg",
  "The 4x4 table for max: a monoid, and the counterexample.")
u:note("The bottom row is one colour repeated four times, and the first colour is")
u:note("missing from it entirely.")
u:note("WHAT TO NOTICE: a repeated colour in a row means two different inputs give the")
u:note("same answer; a missing colour means some answer is unreachable. Both are the")
u:note("same failure seen from opposite sides, and both say the same thing: some")
u:note("element has no inverse -- in a table that is ALREADY associative, the Latin test")
u:note("and the inverse test agree. Without associativity they do not: lesson 11 shows a")
u:note("Latin square with no identity at all.")

u:limits("The Latin shape does NOT prove a table is a group. It is necessary and not sufficient: lesson 11 exhibits a Latin square with no identity and no associativity. It also does not identify WHICH group -- Z4 and Klein wear it identically.")

u:takeaway("A group is four laws, and one of them shows up as a shape: every row and column is a permutation of the elements, because a * b = v has exactly one solution when inverses exist. But read the implication in the right direction. Every group is a Latin square; a Latin square is only a QUASIGROUP, and lesson 11 exhibits one with no identity and no associativity. The shape is necessary, not sufficient -- and it does not even tell you WHICH group: Z4 and the Klein four-group are both Latin squares of order 4, distinguished by something else a picture shows at a glance.")

print("wrote out/08-group-z4.svg:", is_ok(a))
print("wrote out/08-group-klein.svg:", is_ok(b))
print("wrote out/08-not-a-group.svg:", is_ok(c))

# --- Invariants -------------------------------------------------------------
cz = u:classify(z4.table)
ck = u:classify(kl.table)
groups_ok = cz.abelian_group * ck.abelian_group
latin_ok = u:is_latin(z4.table) * u:is_latin(kl.table) * (1 - u:is_latin(mx.table))
tracks_ok = equal(checked, grouped)
distinct_ok = eq(z4_self, 2) * eq(kl_self, 4)
if groups_ok * latin_ok * tracks_ok * distinct_ok * is_ok(a) * is_ok(b) * is_ok(c) {
  ok("a group is a Latin square; Z4 and Klein wear the same shape differently")
} else {
  err("group invariants failed")
}
